ai-qa-git-commit-message = Write Commit Message
ai-qa-git-commit-message-prompt = Write a Conventional Commit message for the staged changes: a subject under 72 characters, a blank line, then a short body saying what changed and why. Put the whole message in a single ```commit fenced block and nothing else.
ai-qa-git-explain-changes = Explain These Changes
ai-qa-git-explain-changes-prompt = Explain what the staged and unstaged changes do, file by file, in plain words.
ai-qa-git-review = Review Before Push
ai-qa-git-review-prompt = Review the staged changes as a careful colleague would before a push: bugs, missing tests, anything that should not be committed (secrets, debug output, large files). Be concrete and brief.
ai-qa-git-resolve-conflict = Resolve This Conflict
ai-qa-git-resolve-conflict-prompt = The conflicted file is in the context. For each conflict block say which side to keep and why, or give the merged text in a fenced block.
ai-qa-git-branch-name = Suggest Branch Name
ai-qa-git-branch-name-prompt = Suggest three short branch names for these changes in the form type/short-description (feature/…, fix/…), and the git command to create the best one in a ```bash block.
ai-qa-git-explain-commit = Explain This Commit
ai-qa-git-explain-commit-prompt = Explain the most recent commit in the log: what it changed and what it was for.
git-ai-tip = Ask about the repository: commit messages, reviews, conflicts, branch names; commands come back in runnable blocks.
git-ai-system = You are helping with a git repository inside HyperStack's Git activity. The context carries the repository name, branch, upstream, `git status --short`, the staged diff (`git diff --cached`, possibly truncated) and the last commits. When you propose git commands, put each in a ```bash fenced block, one command per block, ready to run in the repository root. When asked for a commit message, put it in a single ```commit fenced block.
